 - OMP's own per-message cost is used and marked **exact**. Claude Code costs are **estimated** from the price table (`.ta/pricing.toml`, then the built-in table); where a transcript splits cache writes by lifetime, the 1 hour part is priced at twice the input rate. A model with no price row is **unpriced**: its tokens are counted, its cost is 0 and nothing is invented. Add a row to `.ta/pricing.toml` to price it.
 - The context-size table groups turns by prompt size (input plus cache-read plus cache-creation tokens): 0 to 50k, 50 to 100k, 100 to 200k, 200 to 400k and over 400k. A long session that keeps re-reading a large context shows up as most of its cost in the top rows. TA goals are many turns each and are not in this table.
 
+### Exporting the cost report to Meridian
+
+To analyse cost against your KPIs, write the same report as a Meridian work log:
+
+```bash
+ta cost report --since 2026-10-01 --meridian ~/meridian/cost.jsonl
+meridian analyze --source jsonl --path ~/meridian/cost.jsonl
+```
+
+The first command prints the path, how many records it wrote and the exact `meridian analyze` command to read the file. It writes one record per Claude Code session, OMP session or TA goal, using the same filters as the report (`--since`, `--until`, `--project`). Each record has `id`, `title`, `timestamp`, `tokens_input`, `tokens_output` and `source`, plus string metadata Meridian keeps as-is: `cost_usd`, `cost_exact` (`true` only when every turn's cost was reported by the runtime), `model`, `cache_read_tokens`, `cache_write_tokens`, `context_peak` (largest single-turn prompt, `n/a` for goals) and `workload`. Cache tokens are metadata, not part of `tokens_input`, because they dominate long sessions and are billed at different rates.
+
+Re-running is safe. Records are matched by `id` (`<source>:<session or goal id>`), so a repeated export replaces its own records instead of duplicating them, and records already in the file from other sources are kept. A session whose transcript repeats a message id is counted once, as in the report. The file is replaced atomically, so an interrupted run never leaves half a file. If the existing file has a line that is not valid JSON with an `id`, TA stops and tells you which line, and writes nothing. With `--json`, the report stays on stdout and the export message goes to stderr.
